WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.860
CORS is a browser-enforced sharing policy.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:09.320
It controls when browser JavaScript from one origin can read responses from another origin.

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:18.460
It does not authenticate the caller, authorize the action, validate input, protect non-browser clients, or prove that a request is safe.

4
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.040
Treat CORS as one exposure control in a larger API security design.

5
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:29.480
Allowed origins, methods, headers, and credential settings should be chosen deliberately.

6
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:35.420
Development shortcuts can easily become production exposure if they are copied forward without review.

7
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:47.200
If an API allows credentials, broad origins, broad methods, or broad headers, the team should understand which browser clients are expected and what risk that creates.

8
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.960
Preflight behavior is an operational concern as well as a configuration concern.

9
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:57.500
Browser clients may send OPTIONS requests before certain cross-origin calls.

10
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:06.000
API gateways, load balancers, reverse proxies, authentication middleware, and logging systems need to handle that behavior consistently.

11
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:12.420
Misunderstood preflight handling can create confusing failures that teams work around with overly broad settings.

12
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.020
Different client types have different assumptions.

13
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.160
A browser-based SPA has CORS and cookie concerns.

14
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.020
A mobile app has token storage and platform behavior concerns.

15
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.080
A service client may run without a user present.

16
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.540
A partner integration may have contract, rate, and credential rotation requirements.

17
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.280
API design should not pretend all callers are the same just because they use HTTP.

18
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:43.160
Network placement, CORS, authentication, and authorization are related but distinct.

19
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.260
A private network path does not remove the need for authorization.

20
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.760
CORS does not replace identity.

21
00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:55.460
A gateway can centralize some controls, but the API still needs to enforce the rules it owns.

22
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.960
Confusing these layers creates gaps that are hard to see in code review.

23
00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.760
Client-side controls are useful for usability and guidance, but server-side enforcement protects the system.

24
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:20.580
A web client can hide a button, a mobile app can validate a form, and a generated SDK can guide requests, but the API must still validate inputs, enforce authorization, control resource usage, and return safe errors.
